The lightest option for modern .NET (8+) is **`TimeProvider`** — it's already in the BCL, no NuGet packages or DI containers needed.

### The pattern

1. Accept a `TimeProvider` parameter (defaulting to `TimeProvider.System` so existing callers don't break).
2. In tests, pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (from the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` package) or simply a mock.

### For your code it would look like:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

### In tests:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

// Advance time deterministically:
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30));
```

### Why this is the lightest option

| Approach | Downside |
|----------|----------|
| Custom `IClock` interface | You have to write & maintain it yourself |
| Full DI container | Overkill for a class library |
| **`TimeProvider` (built-in)** | Zero custom code; BCL-supported since .NET 8 |

